started reading the book and solving its exercises, was my curiosity to understand concepts of the random walks’ theory in an applied manner. So, the connection between random walks and electric networks was ideal for me since my undergraduate studies were on electrical and computer engineering. Since the book is freely available on the Internet, I think that this manual will be a nice companion to it. The reader should realize that this work has been done in my free time and I have only considered the first part of the book, namely Random walks on finite networks. Hopefully in the future, either me or maybe someone else will continue this work and solve part’s two exercises (Random walks on infinite networks). Let me also say that the second part of the book is very interesting. It considers infinite networks and an ‘electric’ proof of Pólya’s famous random walk theorem is central to it. Throughout this manual when I refer to ‘the book’, I mean of course the book ‘Random Walks and Electric Networks’. Obviously, probable errors of this manual are my fault and I welcome any constructive observations, comments and corrections to my email: pan_kras@yahoo.gr. My intention is to include them in a next version of this manual.\n\nBy grasping this opportunity, I would like to thank warmly, in chronological order, three people who played a very important role in my journey into science and especially mathematics. The first one is my mathematics Teacher at the last high school class Dr. Efstratios El. Macras, who prepared us to\n\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRandom walks and electric networks (https://studylib.net/doc/8824685/random-walks-and-electric-networks)\nciteturn657search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; Doyle ... Laurie Snell ...     This work is derived from the book Random Walks and Electric Networks, originally published in 1984 by the Mathematical Association of ...     Prefa